WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.318
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.729 --> 00:00:07.270
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: So
today we are going

00:00:07.270 --> 00:00:08.620
to do something interesting.

00:00:08.620 --> 00:00:11.800
We are going to deep dive
into something, come back out,

00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:13.390
go back to the
basics, and then you

00:00:13.390 --> 00:00:15.752
get to question the
people who built

00:00:15.752 --> 00:00:17.710
some of the technologies
I'm going to show you.

00:00:17.710 --> 00:00:19.720
So I have the easy job today.

00:00:19.720 --> 00:00:23.330
So we'll see how that goes.

00:00:23.330 --> 00:00:26.237
So let's start thinking
about some of the trends

00:00:26.237 --> 00:00:28.320
that some of you are seeing
in your own networks--

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:30.560
so they're for
your own services.

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:33.980
One of our cloud customers, for
example, their entire service

00:00:33.980 --> 00:00:36.230
was a single monolith.

00:00:36.230 --> 00:00:38.150
And it used to take
them about three months

00:00:38.150 --> 00:00:40.697
to ship out a single change.

00:00:40.697 --> 00:00:42.530
And what they did is
they took this monolith

00:00:42.530 --> 00:00:46.430
and they chopped it up into
about 80 microservices,

00:00:46.430 --> 00:00:48.290
and the time to
release something

00:00:48.290 --> 00:00:50.780
went down from three
months to a week.

00:00:50.780 --> 00:00:53.210
So it's not that people
care about microservices

00:00:53.210 --> 00:00:56.390
or they care about all of these
technologies, what they really

00:00:56.390 --> 00:01:00.340
care about is what
it brings them.

00:01:00.340 --> 00:01:03.970
The second trend, which you
probably already noticed or are

00:01:03.970 --> 00:01:06.790
facing based on whatever
deployments and services

00:01:06.790 --> 00:01:11.230
that you have, is that hybrid
multicloud, and public cloud,

00:01:11.230 --> 00:01:14.130
and Google Cloud
are here to stay.

00:01:14.130 --> 00:01:16.990
And this could be simply because
you're migrating to the cloud,

00:01:16.990 --> 00:01:19.249
you're not going to make
the migration in one shot,

00:01:19.249 --> 00:01:21.040
or it may be that you
want to actually keep

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:23.140
some of these systems on-prem.

00:01:23.140 --> 00:01:24.670
And then in the
third case, it could

00:01:24.670 --> 00:01:28.120
be that you want to leverage
the best of every public cloud

00:01:28.120 --> 00:01:31.120
in whatever they have to offer,
and so you may be deploying

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:34.500
services on-prem or in a
private cloud, in Google Cloud,

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:36.832
and in another cloud.

00:01:36.832 --> 00:01:39.040
So the thing that we have
been thinking long and hard

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:42.010
this last year is how
can we make it easy

00:01:42.010 --> 00:01:45.450
for you to go deliver hybrid
and multicloud services.

00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:47.920
We are literally not toiling
over the infrastructure that's

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:51.540
actually needed to
make this happen.

00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:53.300
The first thing
that's actually needed

00:01:53.300 --> 00:01:56.720
to make this is to remove
like, look at your service.

00:01:56.720 --> 00:02:00.706
You're essentially crafting each
service piece by piece today.

00:02:00.706 --> 00:02:02.330
And the first thing
that you want to do

00:02:02.330 --> 00:02:05.465
is remove all of the common
core out of your service.

00:02:05.465 --> 00:02:07.340
This would be typically
networking code which

00:02:07.340 --> 00:02:10.699
involves load balancing,
or there's fault injection,

00:02:10.699 --> 00:02:11.660
or failure recovery.

00:02:11.660 --> 00:02:14.190
It depends on what all you're
adding to your services.

00:02:14.190 --> 00:02:15.770
So what if we
removed all of this

00:02:15.770 --> 00:02:18.260
and then encapsulated it
into a simple component

00:02:18.260 --> 00:02:20.270
that you could just
add to your services

00:02:20.270 --> 00:02:22.070
without bothering
to write in all

00:02:22.070 --> 00:02:25.522
of these primitives inside
your services themselves?

00:02:25.522 --> 00:02:27.230
And then the second
thing is, once you've

00:02:27.230 --> 00:02:30.962
done that, who's going to go
manage all of these services.

00:02:30.962 --> 00:02:32.420
And that is where
you need services

00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:33.860
management infrastructure.

00:02:33.860 --> 00:02:37.160
And it's basically not something
you want to manage yourself.

00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:39.440
So you want a fully
managed services

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:42.050
management infrastructure.

00:02:42.050 --> 00:02:44.000
So I thought, let's
do something fun.

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:48.020
Let's actually go and build
out, let's say a summer shop,

00:02:48.020 --> 00:02:51.290
because it's pretty hot-- not in
San Francisco, but other ways.

00:02:51.290 --> 00:02:54.500
And if you think of a
typical web-facing app,

00:02:54.500 --> 00:02:56.690
you'd have a front-end,
and you'd likely

00:02:56.690 --> 00:02:58.340
have a shopping
cart service, which

00:02:58.340 --> 00:02:59.784
is along with your catalog.

00:02:59.784 --> 00:03:01.700
And then you would have
some sort of a payment

00:03:01.700 --> 00:03:04.170
gateway or a payment system.

00:03:04.170 --> 00:03:05.900
So let's call this
our summer shop,

00:03:05.900 --> 00:03:07.670
and we have three services.

00:03:07.670 --> 00:03:09.290
Typically our
user's traffic would

00:03:09.290 --> 00:03:12.210
go in that order, which is
user to front-end to shopping

00:03:12.210 --> 00:03:14.390
to payment.

00:03:14.390 --> 00:03:17.455
Now, one of the nice
things that have actually

00:03:17.455 --> 00:03:19.580
come to the forefront, I
would say, in the last one

00:03:19.580 --> 00:03:21.980
year, or probably a
little bit more than that,

00:03:21.980 --> 00:03:24.710
is this concept
of a Service Mesh.

00:03:24.710 --> 00:03:27.470
And you will hear us talking
about it a bit more as we

00:03:27.470 --> 00:03:29.000
go throughout the session.

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:31.970
But if you had to think of
Service Mesh, in a very simple

00:03:31.970 --> 00:03:34.190
way it's like this--

00:03:34.190 --> 00:03:37.070
imagine your infrastructure
that existed,

00:03:37.070 --> 00:03:39.350
let's say, 10 years
ago, everything used

00:03:39.350 --> 00:03:40.964
to be a one box in project.

00:03:40.964 --> 00:03:43.130
Now, you're doing the same
that services, everything

00:03:43.130 --> 00:03:44.510
is bundled together.

00:03:44.510 --> 00:03:46.010
So what Service
Mesh is trying to do

00:03:46.010 --> 00:03:47.870
is actually decouple that.

00:03:47.870 --> 00:03:51.320
So if you look at your, let's
say it's a part of a VM.

00:03:51.320 --> 00:03:54.500
And on there, all you want
to do is, on your end,

00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:56.720
you just want to write
the service software.

00:03:56.720 --> 00:03:58.580
And what you want
somebody else to do

00:03:58.580 --> 00:04:00.380
is write all of the
common software which

00:04:00.380 --> 00:04:02.510
would be load balancing,
which would be, let's say,

00:04:02.510 --> 00:04:04.030
service authentication.

00:04:04.030 --> 00:04:07.030
Maybe you are doing some sort of
testing, so timeouts, retries--

00:04:07.030 --> 00:04:09.387
all of the failure
recovery mechanisms.

00:04:09.387 --> 00:04:11.720
So that's what you see on the
bottom half of the Service

00:04:11.720 --> 00:04:12.860
Mesh.

00:04:12.860 --> 00:04:14.900
What you see, a very
critical component

00:04:14.900 --> 00:04:16.700
of this as a service
proxy, which we'll

00:04:16.700 --> 00:04:18.200
talk about a bit more.

00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:20.570
That's essentially doing
a lot of the heavy lifting

00:04:20.570 --> 00:04:22.970
of whatever your
services are doing today.

00:04:22.970 --> 00:04:26.570
The service part of it is simply
your either application logic

00:04:26.570 --> 00:04:28.760
or your business logic,
and then the green thing

00:04:28.760 --> 00:04:32.452
that you see at the top is your
Service Mesh Control plane.

00:04:32.452 --> 00:04:34.910
And this is the thing that's
going to talk to your proxies.

00:04:34.910 --> 00:04:37.160
This is the thing that's
going to deliver intelligence

00:04:37.160 --> 00:04:37.932
to your proxies.

00:04:37.932 --> 00:04:39.890
This is the thing that's
going to absorb things

00:04:39.890 --> 00:04:41.720
related to observability.

00:04:41.720 --> 00:04:44.960
So if you had something like
this, as you can imagine,

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:48.410
the green box is all you need
to focus on, and the rest of it

00:04:48.410 --> 00:04:50.270
is taken care of by
your services management

00:04:50.270 --> 00:04:53.360
infrastructure.

00:04:53.360 --> 00:04:55.740
Now, this is where
Envoy comes in.

00:04:55.740 --> 00:04:59.410
And we are really fortunate to
have Matt Klein from Lyft here.

00:04:59.410 --> 00:05:01.980
We will ask him all the
tough questions later.

00:05:01.980 --> 00:05:04.850
But the Envoy proxy is
actually a very key part

00:05:04.850 --> 00:05:06.230
of this infrastructure.

00:05:06.230 --> 00:05:08.120
And if you think of
this service proxy

00:05:08.120 --> 00:05:10.200
that you saw on
the previous slide,

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:13.460
it's basically Enboy that
fulfills that function in here.

00:05:13.460 --> 00:05:16.670
So we use Envoy in addition
to all of the technologies

00:05:16.670 --> 00:05:19.400
that we already have, which is
our Compute Engine, Kubernetes

00:05:19.400 --> 00:05:22.700
Engine, you've heard
of GKE on-prem,

00:05:22.700 --> 00:05:25.710
and then there's our
cloud load balancing.

00:05:25.710 --> 00:05:27.770
So if you took a
closer look at Envoy,

00:05:27.770 --> 00:05:29.780
what is it that you see inside?

00:05:29.780 --> 00:05:32.720
Basically, first of all
this was developed by Lyft.

00:05:32.720 --> 00:05:34.640
And it is also battle
tested at Lyft,

00:05:34.640 --> 00:05:36.650
so it's already
a hardened proxy.

00:05:36.650 --> 00:05:40.010
And the piece that we really
like about Envoy, first of all,

00:05:40.010 --> 00:05:43.100
it is high performance
native call implementation.

00:05:43.100 --> 00:05:46.280
Then it also provides eventually
consistent service discovery.

00:05:46.280 --> 00:05:49.110
And we we'll ask Matt
why that's important.

00:05:49.110 --> 00:05:51.890
Then it's also API driven,
which was really important to us

00:05:51.890 --> 00:05:55.660
because we almost view it
as an integral part of our,

00:05:55.660 --> 00:05:58.160
as you saw, the Service Mesh
and then the service management

00:05:58.160 --> 00:05:58.660
story.

00:05:58.660 --> 00:06:02.100
And it had to be API-driven
for that to be effective.

00:06:02.100 --> 00:06:04.740
It's also extensible with
this notion of filters.

00:06:04.740 --> 00:06:06.920
I'm sure Matt will
touch on it a bit more.

00:06:06.920 --> 00:06:08.870
One of the nice
things is that several

00:06:08.870 --> 00:06:12.830
of these protocols, such as
HTTP/2 are built into Envoy.

00:06:12.830 --> 00:06:14.750
So it supports them
from the get-go.

00:06:14.750 --> 00:06:16.580
So that's again
important, because that's

00:06:16.580 --> 00:06:19.250
what helps you bring in
some of the modernization

00:06:19.250 --> 00:06:22.650
into whichever services
that you have to do.

00:06:22.650 --> 00:06:24.530
And the most
important thing is, it

00:06:24.530 --> 00:06:26.540
was designed for observability.

00:06:26.540 --> 00:06:29.210
A lot of times all of
us add in observability

00:06:29.210 --> 00:06:30.350
as an afterthought.

00:06:30.350 --> 00:06:33.440
And I think that is why a lot
of our services are these silos,

00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:36.590
and we really don't
get an end-to-end view.

00:06:36.590 --> 00:06:38.330
So if you think about
all of this-- now,

00:06:38.330 --> 00:06:40.370
this is what it would
look like if we added

00:06:40.370 --> 00:06:42.790
the proxy into our services.

00:06:42.790 --> 00:06:45.290
And you can see that now each
of each of our services, these

00:06:45.290 --> 00:06:47.180
are either VMs or
containers, whatever

00:06:47.180 --> 00:06:49.040
your favorite stuff is.

00:06:49.040 --> 00:06:50.810
And then we added
a service proxy

00:06:50.810 --> 00:06:52.340
on each of those
instances that's

00:06:52.340 --> 00:06:55.720
running each of those services.

00:06:55.720 --> 00:06:56.950
Now, this is the cool part.

00:06:56.950 --> 00:07:00.930
So we are really excited to
announce Traffic Director.

00:07:00.930 --> 00:07:03.690
Traffic Director
is a new service

00:07:03.690 --> 00:07:06.360
which is going to take away
some of the toil of going

00:07:06.360 --> 00:07:08.410
and managing all
of these services.

00:07:08.410 --> 00:07:11.040
So like I said before, what
you really want to focus on

00:07:11.040 --> 00:07:15.120
is your either application
logic or your business logic,

00:07:15.120 --> 00:07:17.130
and you want Google
to basically take

00:07:17.130 --> 00:07:19.770
care of the rest-- google
and then the service proxy.

00:07:19.770 --> 00:07:22.240
And so that is where we're
going with all of this.

00:07:22.240 --> 00:07:23.950
So this is now
available in alpha.

00:07:23.950 --> 00:07:27.710
You are the first people who
are actually hearing about it.

00:07:27.710 --> 00:07:29.750
So what exactly is it?

00:07:29.750 --> 00:07:32.580
First of all, it's a
GCP-managed service.

00:07:32.580 --> 00:07:35.930
Then you can think of it almost
as an enterprise ready control

00:07:35.930 --> 00:07:38.770
plane for all of the service
proxies we spoke about.

00:07:38.770 --> 00:07:42.260
And the nice thing is that
you had a Service Mesh which

00:07:42.260 --> 00:07:44.510
could extend to a
zone, we are going

00:07:44.510 --> 00:07:47.970
to let you extend your
Service Mesh across regions.

00:07:47.970 --> 00:07:50.900
So you are very used to
our global load balancing

00:07:50.900 --> 00:07:52.130
with a single [INAUDIBLE].

00:07:52.130 --> 00:07:55.970
Imagine that you brought that
same concept to microservices,

00:07:55.970 --> 00:07:58.239
and then you have the
same things that you

00:07:58.239 --> 00:07:59.780
get with global load
balancing as you

00:07:59.780 --> 00:08:01.760
get with our external one.

00:08:01.760 --> 00:08:03.780
The other nice thing is
that Traffic Director

00:08:03.780 --> 00:08:07.670
will also health check all of
your proxies for you, which

00:08:07.670 --> 00:08:10.260
means each proxy doesn't need
to health check each other.

00:08:10.260 --> 00:08:14.030
So you have [INAUDIBLE] square
health checking problem,

00:08:14.030 --> 00:08:15.700
and you've just
simplified and outsource

00:08:15.700 --> 00:08:17.270
that to the Traffic Director.

00:08:17.270 --> 00:08:19.040
So I would say the
two main things

00:08:19.040 --> 00:08:21.520
that you want to think about
are global load balancing,

00:08:21.520 --> 00:08:23.270
health checking
at scale, and then

00:08:23.270 --> 00:08:26.660
the third thing is auto
scaling, which is typically

00:08:26.660 --> 00:08:28.740
available for all
of our services.

00:08:28.740 --> 00:08:32.120
So based on traffic signals
this will go and actually

00:08:32.120 --> 00:08:33.642
scaling back-ends
as well, whichever

00:08:33.642 --> 00:08:34.850
ones are running the service.

00:08:37.789 --> 00:08:41.820
So if we add a Traffic Detector,
that is where it will go to.

00:08:41.820 --> 00:08:43.760
One of the things that
we really wanted to do

00:08:43.760 --> 00:08:46.340
was to use open API
between the Traffic

00:08:46.340 --> 00:08:48.590
Director and the service proxy.

00:08:48.590 --> 00:08:51.200
And so we use the
exteriors, we do APIs.

00:08:51.200 --> 00:08:53.400
And we'll talk
about those as well.

00:08:53.400 --> 00:08:54.950
And then if you look
at this picture,

00:08:54.950 --> 00:08:57.530
essentially the green
box is managed by us,

00:08:57.530 --> 00:08:59.060
and then the proxy
is the one that's

00:08:59.060 --> 00:09:01.280
taking all of your
networking code.

00:09:01.280 --> 00:09:03.950
And then it's sitting next to
your application or business

00:09:03.950 --> 00:09:08.990
logic and making things
much, much simpler for you.

00:09:08.990 --> 00:09:11.990
So I spoke a little bit
about global load balancing.

00:09:11.990 --> 00:09:14.340
And what does that
exactly look like?

00:09:14.340 --> 00:09:16.800
So this is what we are
going to go and look at,

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:19.090
which is the service
that we spoke about.

00:09:19.090 --> 00:09:20.950
I have three services--
the web front-end,

00:09:20.950 --> 00:09:22.850
the shopping cart,
and the payment.

00:09:22.850 --> 00:09:25.150
If you can see the first
block, that is, essentially,

00:09:25.150 --> 00:09:26.776
your web front-end.

00:09:26.776 --> 00:09:28.400
And these are not
two separate things--

00:09:28.400 --> 00:09:31.700
one sitting in Singapore region,
and one sitting somewhere

00:09:31.700 --> 00:09:32.600
in the US--

00:09:32.600 --> 00:09:35.480
this is actually one service
with just instances sitting

00:09:35.480 --> 00:09:37.520
in these two different regions.

00:09:37.520 --> 00:09:40.700
Same for shopping cart, and
then same for the last service,

00:09:40.700 --> 00:09:41.840
which is payment.

00:09:41.840 --> 00:09:43.610
So what did we do so far?

00:09:43.610 --> 00:09:47.060
We put in a proxy, we
put in Traffic Director.

00:09:47.060 --> 00:09:49.250
And the moment we put
in Traffic Director,

00:09:49.250 --> 00:09:53.100
we could go expand our service
beyond a single region.

00:09:53.100 --> 00:09:54.740
Otherwise, if you
did it on your own,

00:09:54.740 --> 00:09:57.140
you would pretty much
be in a single region.

00:09:57.140 --> 00:09:59.150
But Traffic Director
enables you to go

00:09:59.150 --> 00:10:03.990
and then spillover, or even
failover across regions.

00:10:03.990 --> 00:10:06.410
So, I thought, let's take a
quick look at a live setup.

00:10:06.410 --> 00:10:10.820
I'm going to ask our folks
to switch me to the demo.

00:10:10.820 --> 00:10:14.270
And we will also pray
to the demo gods.

00:10:14.270 --> 00:10:16.100
Can you-- OK.

00:10:16.100 --> 00:10:19.940
So this is, essentially,
our summer shop.

00:10:19.940 --> 00:10:23.630
And we have got some interesting
things that we want to sell,

00:10:23.630 --> 00:10:25.940
including, I just
noticed this morning,

00:10:25.940 --> 00:10:29.930
a very expensive popsicle,
which costs 300 bucks.

00:10:29.930 --> 00:10:34.040
But what you're seeing here
really is that, remember,

00:10:34.040 --> 00:10:35.840
we talked about
the three services?

00:10:35.840 --> 00:10:39.170
And the first service is the
one that is in US central.

00:10:39.170 --> 00:10:41.540
This is our front-end service.

00:10:41.540 --> 00:10:44.180
Now, let's try and let's buy
the expensive popsicle now

00:10:44.180 --> 00:10:45.440
that we talked about it.

00:10:45.440 --> 00:10:49.610
So if you now click this
one, and what you notice

00:10:49.610 --> 00:10:52.310
is there's this small blob here
which is a little bit cut off,

00:10:52.310 --> 00:10:54.080
but that's our
shopping cart service.

00:10:54.080 --> 00:10:57.020
Again, the instances here
are the ones in US central.

00:10:57.020 --> 00:10:59.630
And the reason is
because I'm right here.

00:10:59.630 --> 00:11:02.240
And our other set of
instances were in Singapore,

00:11:02.240 --> 00:11:05.180
so obviously the instances in
US Central, which is in Iowa,

00:11:05.180 --> 00:11:07.290
are closer to me.

00:11:07.290 --> 00:11:10.550
And then, obviously, we have
a lot of money to spend.

00:11:10.550 --> 00:11:12.980
So let's go by
the $300 popsicle.

00:11:12.980 --> 00:11:14.870
And what you will notice
is that the payment

00:11:14.870 --> 00:11:16.490
service that it
went to is actually

00:11:16.490 --> 00:11:18.600
this us-central1-c as well.

00:11:18.600 --> 00:11:20.750
So if you go back to
the diagram I just

00:11:20.750 --> 00:11:23.240
showed you before,
and let me show

00:11:23.240 --> 00:11:25.620
you the back-end of this a bit.

00:11:25.620 --> 00:11:29.150
Now, one of the things
about the way we set up is--

00:11:29.150 --> 00:11:30.510
let's go back in here.

00:11:30.510 --> 00:11:32.300
So these are all
the instance groups.

00:11:32.300 --> 00:11:34.970
These are literally the
managed instance groups

00:11:34.970 --> 00:11:36.840
running each of your services.

00:11:36.840 --> 00:11:40.550
So what you're seeing in here,
essentially, is the first thing

00:11:40.550 --> 00:11:42.500
that you would see is
our front-end, which

00:11:42.500 --> 00:11:43.562
is two instance groups.

00:11:43.562 --> 00:11:45.020
And the second
thing, you would see

00:11:45.020 --> 00:11:47.750
our cart, which is two instance
groups, one in each region.

00:11:47.750 --> 00:11:49.708
And then the third thing
would be our payment--

00:11:49.708 --> 00:11:50.907
again, one in each region.

00:11:50.907 --> 00:11:52.490
And the last thing
is simply because I

00:11:52.490 --> 00:11:55.200
want to masquerade as a
client from Singapore--

00:11:55.200 --> 00:11:56.980
so that's just one
setting in there.

00:11:56.980 --> 00:11:58.730
But what you would
really want to look at,

00:11:58.730 --> 00:12:00.380
is go to network
services and then

00:12:00.380 --> 00:12:02.600
look at you load balancing,
and this is where

00:12:02.600 --> 00:12:04.230
you would see three services.

00:12:04.230 --> 00:12:06.920
And if we go actually
to the back-ends,

00:12:06.920 --> 00:12:09.680
you can see that this is
the front-end service,

00:12:09.680 --> 00:12:14.000
then the biggest one is
the shopping cart service,

00:12:14.000 --> 00:12:16.832
and then the last one
is the payment service.

00:12:16.832 --> 00:12:19.040
So this is what it looked
like when you were actually

00:12:19.040 --> 00:12:20.717
accessing it from here.

00:12:20.717 --> 00:12:22.800
And then the last thing
that we would want to do--

00:12:22.800 --> 00:12:24.680
we can try it live and see.

00:12:24.680 --> 00:12:27.570
So I wanted to literally
do everything from scratch.

00:12:27.570 --> 00:12:30.200
So this service now,
this is basically

00:12:30.200 --> 00:12:33.000
a client that's going
to sit in Singapore.

00:12:33.000 --> 00:12:38.070
And-- so maybe
it's not connected.

00:12:38.070 --> 00:12:40.530
So there is a client that's
sitting in Singapore.

00:12:40.530 --> 00:12:42.470
And what we really
want to see in here is,

00:12:42.470 --> 00:12:45.000
would the client in Singapore
actually go in there?

00:12:45.000 --> 00:12:47.400
And we'll see if it
wants to go there or not.

00:12:47.400 --> 00:12:50.950
If not, we'll see
it on the console.

00:12:50.950 --> 00:12:54.140
So I think that might be
because it's their setup.

00:12:54.140 --> 00:12:56.330
But let's go back and
take a look at this one.

00:12:56.330 --> 00:12:58.900
So I wanted to actually
take you back again

00:12:58.900 --> 00:13:02.680
to when we in the summer shop,
if you looked at this one here.

00:13:02.680 --> 00:13:06.550
What you can see in here
is these instances--

00:13:06.550 --> 00:13:08.570
the payment in South Asia.

00:13:08.570 --> 00:13:11.170
So when we come in as a
client from South Asia,

00:13:11.170 --> 00:13:14.200
you would go back and first hit
the front-end in South Asia,

00:13:14.200 --> 00:13:16.570
and then you would hit the
shopping cart in South Asia,

00:13:16.570 --> 00:13:19.630
and then you would hit the
payment service in South Asia.

00:13:19.630 --> 00:13:21.615
So I wanted to switch
back to the slides,

00:13:21.615 --> 00:13:23.740
because I wanted to show
what that would look like.

00:13:27.556 --> 00:13:28.680
Can we get the slides back?

00:13:33.430 --> 00:13:34.760
OK.

00:13:34.760 --> 00:13:36.390
So why is that important?

00:13:36.390 --> 00:13:38.390
And the reason it
is important is

00:13:38.390 --> 00:13:42.850
for this reason, which is if
something were to happen to,

00:13:42.850 --> 00:13:46.190
let's say, all of your
instances in the middle region,

00:13:46.190 --> 00:13:48.080
if something were
to happen to that,

00:13:48.080 --> 00:13:50.870
your first service can
actually go and failover

00:13:50.870 --> 00:13:52.370
to the shopping
cart that's actually

00:13:52.370 --> 00:13:54.489
deployed in the second region.

00:13:54.489 --> 00:13:56.030
And then let's say
there is something

00:13:56.030 --> 00:13:58.488
that happens with the shopping
cart service in that region,

00:13:58.488 --> 00:14:02.690
because, for instance, it's
just not able to scale up.

00:14:02.690 --> 00:14:04.910
Then it would go and it
can access the service

00:14:04.910 --> 00:14:06.450
that's in the other region.

00:14:06.450 --> 00:14:09.290
So just like you're used to
global load balancing, which

00:14:09.290 --> 00:14:11.360
is available for
external services,

00:14:11.360 --> 00:14:13.740
you get the same capabilities
with Traffic Director

00:14:13.740 --> 00:14:17.300
for your own internal
services as well.

00:14:17.300 --> 00:14:21.319
And this is some of the other
config that I abstracted out.

00:14:21.319 --> 00:14:23.360
You generally won't have
to configure all of this

00:14:23.360 --> 00:14:25.520
by the time you folks
get to using this.

00:14:25.520 --> 00:14:28.940
But this is some under the
hood configuration, where

00:14:28.940 --> 00:14:31.880
you essentially set up Traffic
Director for your services,

00:14:31.880 --> 00:14:34.640
and then you also went, and
if you see at the bottom

00:14:34.640 --> 00:14:36.770
you can see Envoy is
running on an instance.

00:14:36.770 --> 00:14:40.840
So this is just a snip of
one of those instances.

00:14:40.840 --> 00:14:42.810
So we spoke about all
of this, and now we're

00:14:42.810 --> 00:14:44.660
going to speak about
something that you

00:14:44.660 --> 00:14:46.784
have been hearing throughout
this conference, which

00:14:46.784 --> 00:14:48.480
is Istio.

00:14:48.480 --> 00:14:51.912
And if you look
closely at Istio--

00:14:51.912 --> 00:14:53.370
I think all of you
are mostly going

00:14:53.370 --> 00:14:55.230
to be familiar with Istio--

00:14:55.230 --> 00:14:56.820
what you get is
service discovery,

00:14:56.820 --> 00:14:59.070
you get security
instrumentation,

00:14:59.070 --> 00:15:01.470
you get client-side
load balancing.

00:15:01.470 --> 00:15:03.780
For those of us who have come
from the traditional load

00:15:03.780 --> 00:15:05.991
balancing world, it
is a bit of a change

00:15:05.991 --> 00:15:08.490
because you're always used to
the server-side doing the load

00:15:08.490 --> 00:15:11.670
balancing, and here you
have the client-side

00:15:11.670 --> 00:15:14.130
that has the intelligence
to pick the right back-end.

00:15:14.130 --> 00:15:15.630
And then, of course,
you have things

00:15:15.630 --> 00:15:17.129
such as circuit
breaking, and you've

00:15:17.129 --> 00:15:19.690
got dynamic routing,
and so on and so forth.

00:15:19.690 --> 00:15:22.350
Again, building on the
theme of observability,

00:15:22.350 --> 00:15:25.620
you can do observability
end-to-end using Istio.

00:15:25.620 --> 00:15:27.930
And then you obviously
have mTLS-based security

00:15:27.930 --> 00:15:29.310
and several of
the other features

00:15:29.310 --> 00:15:32.050
that you'll hear talked
about in these sessions.

00:15:32.050 --> 00:15:33.600
One of the nice
things about Istio

00:15:33.600 --> 00:15:36.120
is that you can use it
with Kubernetes or GKE,

00:15:36.120 --> 00:15:38.190
but you can also
use it with VMs.

00:15:38.190 --> 00:15:41.040
So as you go transition
your infrastructure,

00:15:41.040 --> 00:15:44.370
you can actually also go and
use it as a migration tool.

00:15:44.370 --> 00:15:47.166
So you could pretty much
set this up with your VMs.

00:15:47.166 --> 00:15:48.790
And then as you chop
up your monolith--

00:15:48.790 --> 00:15:50.760
so as you containerize
your services--

00:15:50.760 --> 00:15:54.470
you could just move
them piece by piece.

00:15:54.470 --> 00:15:56.540
And then the other
thing is, this

00:15:56.540 --> 00:15:59.250
is what Istio an Envoy
look like together.

00:15:59.250 --> 00:16:01.730
So same model, there's
a control plane,

00:16:01.730 --> 00:16:04.200
there's a data plane, which
is with service A, service

00:16:04.200 --> 00:16:06.090
B, and then the proxy there.

00:16:06.090 --> 00:16:08.000
So typically, we
use Envoy proxy,

00:16:08.000 --> 00:16:12.320
but any XDS with your
compliant proxy can be used.

00:16:12.320 --> 00:16:14.600
Now, if you just
imagine this picture,

00:16:14.600 --> 00:16:17.780
and you put Traffic Director in
the mix, what you will notice

00:16:17.780 --> 00:16:19.940
is this, that
whatever capabilities

00:16:19.940 --> 00:16:23.580
you get out of pilot, you can
put Traffic Director there.

00:16:23.580 --> 00:16:25.580
And that is one of
the main reasons

00:16:25.580 --> 00:16:27.680
we chose to keep the
interface between Traffic

00:16:27.680 --> 00:16:30.590
Director and the proxies
using open source APIs.

00:16:30.590 --> 00:16:32.570
Because in the
future, you will be

00:16:32.570 --> 00:16:36.020
able to just go substitute
your open source pilot using

00:16:36.020 --> 00:16:39.020
the Traffic Director.

00:16:39.020 --> 00:16:42.500
And then, if there is one
takeaway you probably took away

00:16:42.500 --> 00:16:45.140
from this conference, it's
that Google and Google

00:16:45.140 --> 00:16:47.150
Cloud are all-in on hybrid.

00:16:47.150 --> 00:16:49.160
And so we want your
hybrid services

00:16:49.160 --> 00:16:53.390
to get the same benefits that
any of your GCP services get,

00:16:53.390 --> 00:16:56.360
which is specifically as it
relates to services management

00:16:56.360 --> 00:16:57.560
infrastructure.

00:16:57.560 --> 00:17:00.260
And so we want to provide
Traffic Director such

00:17:00.260 --> 00:17:02.210
that it can not only
manage the proxies,

00:17:02.210 --> 00:17:04.490
and talk to the proxies,
and provide global load

00:17:04.490 --> 00:17:07.170
balancing for things in Google
Cloud, but also for things

00:17:07.170 --> 00:17:07.670
on-prem.

00:17:10.650 --> 00:17:12.630
And then this is the
picture you probably

00:17:12.630 --> 00:17:15.240
saw on the keynote day, which
is bringing all of these pieces

00:17:15.240 --> 00:17:16.300
together.

00:17:16.300 --> 00:17:19.260
And this is how Traffic
Director, Envoy, and then

00:17:19.260 --> 00:17:22.170
some our existing technologies,
such as multi-cluster Ingress,

00:17:22.170 --> 00:17:24.720
which under the hood has
our global load balancer.

00:17:24.720 --> 00:17:26.069
All of these fit in together.

00:17:26.069 --> 00:17:29.010
Because at the end of the
day, what you really need

00:17:29.010 --> 00:17:31.620
is not point products
that you stitch together.

00:17:31.620 --> 00:17:34.680
You need a whole suite of
products, or rather a platform,

00:17:34.680 --> 00:17:37.164
that can help you go
and build whatever

00:17:37.164 --> 00:17:38.830
services you want to
build, whether it's

00:17:38.830 --> 00:17:40.830
on VMs, whether it's on
containers, whether it's

00:17:40.830 --> 00:17:43.170
on-prem in GCP and other cloud.

00:17:43.170 --> 00:17:45.780
You should be able to do
it the same way with ease

00:17:45.780 --> 00:17:48.900
in a way that takes away
all the toil from your end.

00:17:48.900 --> 00:17:52.920
So I took you through a
whirlwind tour of all of this.

00:17:52.920 --> 00:17:56.580
I now want to start with
where this all began.

00:17:56.580 --> 00:17:58.520
And so, please join
me in welcoming

00:17:58.520 --> 00:18:02.760
Anna Verenberg from Google,
and Matt Klein from Lyft,

00:18:02.760 --> 00:18:05.910
who I get the pleasure of asking
really difficult questions.

00:18:05.910 --> 00:18:08.820
After our talk, they will
be available outside to talk

00:18:08.820 --> 00:18:11.056
to you, so feel free to
bring in your questions.

00:18:11.056 --> 00:18:12.930
And I also wanted to
give a special shout out

00:18:12.930 --> 00:18:15.400
to the people who helped
me build these questions.

00:18:15.400 --> 00:18:17.340
These are all crowdsourced.

00:18:17.340 --> 00:18:20.340
So I'm going to probably
take one of these.

00:18:20.340 --> 00:18:21.890
Do you folks have a mic?

00:18:21.890 --> 00:18:24.480
OK, I'll take this one.

00:18:24.480 --> 00:18:28.550
So let's start with the most
popular question I got asked.

00:18:28.550 --> 00:18:29.910
So maybe let's start with Matt.

00:18:29.910 --> 00:18:32.250
Matt, why don't you
tell us about yourself.

00:18:32.250 --> 00:18:34.410
But the popular
question was not that--

00:18:34.410 --> 00:18:40.170
it was, tell everybody one thing
that they don't know about you.

00:18:40.170 --> 00:18:41.190
MATT KLEIN: OK.

00:18:41.190 --> 00:18:41.830
Hi, everyone.

00:18:41.830 --> 00:18:43.530
Thanks for having me.

00:18:43.530 --> 00:18:45.240
I'm Matt, obviously.

00:18:45.240 --> 00:18:48.300
I've been Lyft for
about 3 and 1/2 years,

00:18:48.300 --> 00:18:49.770
where I work on networking.

00:18:49.770 --> 00:18:52.620
Prior to Lyft I worked at
Twitter, also in networking.

00:18:52.620 --> 00:18:55.580
Before that, I worked
at Amazon on EC2.

00:18:55.580 --> 00:18:57.330
So I've been working
in the infrastructure

00:18:57.330 --> 00:19:01.210
space for over 10 years.

00:19:01.210 --> 00:19:04.780
Let's see, one thing that
people don't know about me.

00:19:04.780 --> 00:19:07.470
I have a very unhealthy
obsession with pickles.

00:19:07.470 --> 00:19:11.430
And I've been known to eat
so many pickles that then I

00:19:11.430 --> 00:19:12.990
have to go drink
a bunch of water

00:19:12.990 --> 00:19:15.900
because I have like
a salt overdose.

00:19:15.900 --> 00:19:17.760
So don't give me pickles.

00:19:20.640 --> 00:19:22.680
ANNA BERENBERG: Hi, my
name is Anna Berenberg,

00:19:22.680 --> 00:19:28.740
and I've been a software
engineer for 27 years.

00:19:28.740 --> 00:19:32.130
In the last 20 years plus,
I work in the networking

00:19:32.130 --> 00:19:35.640
industry, and I never wrote
a line of networking code.

00:19:35.640 --> 00:19:40.620
I always had interest in
building fault tolerant,

00:19:40.620 --> 00:19:42.720
high availability
distributed systems,

00:19:42.720 --> 00:19:45.760
and process management, system
management, stuff like that.

00:19:45.760 --> 00:19:49.560
And then I joined Google
12 and 1/2 years ago

00:19:49.560 --> 00:19:53.100
and spent the last nine
years in infrastructure

00:19:53.100 --> 00:19:54.390
and technical infrastructure.

00:19:54.390 --> 00:19:57.960
And without moving my
dust, I moved into cloud

00:19:57.960 --> 00:20:01.050
three years ago, becoming
responsible for all cloud load

00:20:01.050 --> 00:20:04.770
balancing and Google
load balancing products.

00:20:04.770 --> 00:20:08.050
One thing people
don't know about me,

00:20:08.050 --> 00:20:12.629
I used to dance professionally
ballroom dancing.

00:20:12.629 --> 00:20:14.920
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: We might have
time for that at the end.

00:20:14.920 --> 00:20:18.750
[LAUGHTER]

00:20:18.750 --> 00:20:21.120
And you guys have the mic, OK.

00:20:21.120 --> 00:20:23.580
So actually this
question is for you Matt.

00:20:23.580 --> 00:20:26.010
Let's start with you,
because I generally

00:20:26.010 --> 00:20:29.220
assume that most people
would know about Envoy

00:20:29.220 --> 00:20:31.320
and what the journey was.

00:20:31.320 --> 00:20:33.960
But what we really wanted
to do was, a lot of people

00:20:33.960 --> 00:20:37.110
were very interested in hearing
the journey in your own words.

00:20:37.110 --> 00:20:41.490
So how did Envoy start, and
what were the exact problems

00:20:41.490 --> 00:20:42.900
you were trying to solve?

00:20:42.900 --> 00:20:44.460
How did it all begin?

00:20:44.460 --> 00:20:45.390
MATT KLEIN: Sure.

00:20:45.390 --> 00:20:48.360
So I've been working on
service architectures

00:20:48.360 --> 00:20:52.590
for quite some time, like I
was saying, over 10 years now.

00:20:52.590 --> 00:20:55.570
So I've seen how Amazon
had kind of solved

00:20:55.570 --> 00:20:57.570
these different problems
that people are facing,

00:20:57.570 --> 00:20:59.800
how it had been done
over at Twitter,

00:20:59.800 --> 00:21:03.210
and I think what I mostly tell
people is most people that

00:21:03.210 --> 00:21:06.790
are rolling out microservice
architectures, it sounds great,

00:21:06.790 --> 00:21:08.490
we're obviously
told to go and do it

00:21:08.490 --> 00:21:10.230
for all of the human
scaling benefits,

00:21:10.230 --> 00:21:13.860
but we sometimes gloss over
all the complexities that

00:21:13.860 --> 00:21:15.250
come from actually doing that.

00:21:15.250 --> 00:21:18.147
So I think a lot of smaller
companies that aren't Google,

00:21:18.147 --> 00:21:20.730
they go and they start to roll
out microservice architectures,

00:21:20.730 --> 00:21:23.190
and they wind up in
a situation where

00:21:23.190 --> 00:21:25.410
they might get 10
or 20 services,

00:21:25.410 --> 00:21:27.550
and then things start
getting really difficult,

00:21:27.550 --> 00:21:30.630
because they're facing
common problems in networking

00:21:30.630 --> 00:21:32.280
with observability.

00:21:32.280 --> 00:21:34.630
And what we found
at Lyft, which is

00:21:34.630 --> 00:21:36.300
what I think a lot
of companies face,

00:21:36.300 --> 00:21:38.120
is that they go
from their model,

00:21:38.120 --> 00:21:40.620
and if they start rolling
out microservices,

00:21:40.620 --> 00:21:44.250
and then the developers don't
trust the actual microservice

00:21:44.250 --> 00:21:44.940
platform.

00:21:44.940 --> 00:21:47.221
And you wind up in this
horrible situation where

00:21:47.221 --> 00:21:49.470
you have services that people
don't trust because they

00:21:49.470 --> 00:21:52.470
can't debug them, they don't
understand what's going on.

00:21:52.470 --> 00:21:55.230
And then they start adding
code to their monolith again.

00:21:55.230 --> 00:21:57.390
So you're in you're in
this awful situation

00:21:57.390 --> 00:22:00.120
of having services that
people don't want to use

00:22:00.120 --> 00:22:04.080
and continuing to actually
use your monolith.

00:22:04.080 --> 00:22:06.900
So the way that people
have historically

00:22:06.900 --> 00:22:09.440
solved this problem
is using libraries.

00:22:09.440 --> 00:22:12.400
So at Twitter there was
a library called Finagle.

00:22:12.400 --> 00:22:15.177
Google obviously has a
library called Stubby.

00:22:15.177 --> 00:22:17.260
And there's other companies
that have solved this.

00:22:17.260 --> 00:22:19.690
And that library does a
bunch of common concerns

00:22:19.690 --> 00:22:21.400
around things like
observability,

00:22:21.400 --> 00:22:24.700
load balancing,
retries, timeouts.

00:22:24.700 --> 00:22:27.820
But the industry has moved
to a polyglot architecture.

00:22:27.820 --> 00:22:30.130
And by that, I mean
that the days of Java

00:22:30.130 --> 00:22:31.990
only are basically over.

00:22:31.990 --> 00:22:35.300
So people now are building
services in five, six, seven,

00:22:35.300 --> 00:22:37.330
eight different languages.

00:22:37.330 --> 00:22:40.420
So building a library in those
eight different languages

00:22:40.420 --> 00:22:44.777
that your company has to
support is kind of a nonstarter.

00:22:44.777 --> 00:22:46.360
So when I joined
Lyft, Lyft was having

00:22:46.360 --> 00:22:48.640
all of these typical
problems around networking,

00:22:48.640 --> 00:22:50.980
observability, load
balancing, this kind

00:22:50.980 --> 00:22:54.190
of aborted microservice rollout.

00:22:54.190 --> 00:22:58.510
And I knew that we could
do a better solution.

00:22:58.510 --> 00:23:02.980
And that was this out of
process proxy system where

00:23:02.980 --> 00:23:05.560
we could put all of the
complicated logic in there

00:23:05.560 --> 00:23:08.410
and provide that for every
application language.

00:23:08.410 --> 00:23:10.510
And it kind of grew from there.

00:23:10.510 --> 00:23:13.000
It became very popular at Lyft.

00:23:13.000 --> 00:23:15.250
It was solving a lot
of business problems.

00:23:15.250 --> 00:23:18.850
And we open sourced Envoy
about two years ago,

00:23:18.850 --> 00:23:21.970
and nowhere in my
wildest dreams would

00:23:21.970 --> 00:23:24.527
I have imagined that I'd
be sitting here right now

00:23:24.527 --> 00:23:26.110
and we'd be announcing
these products.

00:23:26.110 --> 00:23:27.580
It's been pretty incredible.

00:23:27.580 --> 00:23:30.530
But envoy has been sweeping
across the industry,

00:23:30.530 --> 00:23:33.070
so there's been a
lot of other folks

00:23:33.070 --> 00:23:34.820
that have had common problems.

00:23:34.820 --> 00:23:37.450
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: So did you
just decide to open source it?

00:23:37.450 --> 00:23:41.014
Was there any big agenda?

00:23:41.014 --> 00:23:42.430
MATT KLEIN: There's
no big agenda.

00:23:42.430 --> 00:23:47.500
But to be totally honest, this
is kind of the second time

00:23:47.500 --> 00:23:50.260
that I've built Envoy, at
least to a certain degree.

00:23:50.260 --> 00:23:52.420
We did something
similar back at Twitter.

00:23:52.420 --> 00:23:55.500
And I don't want to
build this again.

00:23:55.500 --> 00:23:56.470
[LAUGHTER]

00:23:56.470 --> 00:23:58.930
So two times is enough.

00:23:58.930 --> 00:23:59.860
Yeah.

00:23:59.860 --> 00:24:01.720
PRAJAKTA JOSHI:
That's a good reason.

00:24:01.720 --> 00:24:05.080
So Matt, you bring
a lot of expertise

00:24:05.080 --> 00:24:07.360
as you built out
Envoy and so on.

00:24:07.360 --> 00:24:09.730
I know Anna personally,
because I work with her

00:24:09.730 --> 00:24:11.740
on a day-to-day
basis, and I've seen

00:24:11.740 --> 00:24:15.310
her do something similar as it
relates to services at Google.

00:24:15.310 --> 00:24:18.700
And maybe, Anna, I think what
would be nice is if you can--

00:24:18.700 --> 00:24:20.860
you've been there at
Google for 12 plus years,

00:24:20.860 --> 00:24:24.490
and some of the experiences that
you had building out services.

00:24:24.490 --> 00:24:26.440
But then also about Istio--

00:24:26.440 --> 00:24:29.770
how did we decide to open
Istio and how does it

00:24:29.770 --> 00:24:32.380
fit in with Kubernetes and
several of our other services

00:24:32.380 --> 00:24:33.980
as well?

00:24:33.980 --> 00:24:37.750
ANNA BERENBERG: So building,
I worked in the last 12 years

00:24:37.750 --> 00:24:38.250
actually.

00:24:38.250 --> 00:24:42.220
I wanted to work
on Gmail, instead

00:24:42.220 --> 00:24:46.030
I got assigned to group
doing load balancing.

00:24:46.030 --> 00:24:48.130
And I thought, fine,
I'll do one year

00:24:48.130 --> 00:24:50.400
and I'll go do something else.

00:24:50.400 --> 00:24:51.710
I'm still there.

00:24:51.710 --> 00:24:52.210
Why?

00:24:52.210 --> 00:24:55.060
Because it's such a
fascinating problem.

00:24:55.060 --> 00:24:56.860
The way we solved
it at Google, it's

00:24:56.860 --> 00:25:02.110
actually a control system that
does a pretty sophisticated

00:25:02.110 --> 00:25:06.700
service that does global
load balancing in a way that

00:25:06.700 --> 00:25:10.750
improves quality of life for
the services we build for.

00:25:10.750 --> 00:25:16.270
For example, how do you
protect against query of death?

00:25:16.270 --> 00:25:21.330
How do you figure out that not
all queries or requests add

00:25:21.330 --> 00:25:23.920
the same cost,
because some queries

00:25:23.920 --> 00:25:25.450
are more expensive than others?

00:25:25.450 --> 00:25:29.770
How do you make sure your
back-ends will never overload?

00:25:29.770 --> 00:25:32.680
All of that needs to
be thought through so

00:25:32.680 --> 00:25:35.570
that services developers
don't have to.

00:25:35.570 --> 00:25:38.320
And they don't have to build
some sophisticated solution.

00:25:38.320 --> 00:25:42.940
All they have to do is worry
about the business logic.

00:25:42.940 --> 00:25:45.430
So with all of that,
my focus has always

00:25:45.430 --> 00:25:50.020
been on the control
side of load balancing.

00:25:50.020 --> 00:25:54.460
And so, when I saw
Matt's Envoy proxy,

00:25:54.460 --> 00:25:58.330
I realized that this is exactly
the proxy I wanted to have.

00:25:58.330 --> 00:25:58.830
Why?

00:25:58.830 --> 00:26:01.150
Because it's API-drive.

00:26:01.150 --> 00:26:07.840
And it's funny, it
had very similar APIs

00:26:07.840 --> 00:26:12.970
to the APIs we had internally
at Google between the control

00:26:12.970 --> 00:26:18.850
plane system which does
global load dancing

00:26:18.850 --> 00:26:21.400
and the proxies themselves.

00:26:21.400 --> 00:26:26.520
So with that, when the
idea of Istio came out--

00:26:26.520 --> 00:26:34.100
sorry-- and Traffic Director--

00:26:34.100 --> 00:26:44.160
thank you-- became a natural
substitution for a pilot

00:26:44.160 --> 00:26:48.720
because, with the same APIs
now, we can build into Istio

00:26:48.720 --> 00:26:52.910
and make Istio available
not only within a single GKE

00:26:52.910 --> 00:26:55.890
cluster, but make it horizontal
and make it available

00:26:55.890 --> 00:27:00.240
across the whole GCP, and
on-prem and multicloud.

00:27:00.240 --> 00:27:03.480
And in multicloud, when we talk
about global load balancing,

00:27:03.480 --> 00:27:05.570
first we were talking
about Google services,

00:27:05.570 --> 00:27:08.910
then we started talking global
load balancing within a GCP.

00:27:08.910 --> 00:27:11.040
And now, when we
are saying global,

00:27:11.040 --> 00:27:14.760
it means really global,
worldwide no matter where.

00:27:14.760 --> 00:27:16.680
That's where our aspiration is.

00:27:16.680 --> 00:27:18.360
Today we don't
have it, but that's

00:27:18.360 --> 00:27:20.500
where we are going with it.

00:27:20.500 --> 00:27:22.650
We're going to take
Traffic Director

00:27:22.650 --> 00:27:26.880
and make it load balance
anything, anywhere

00:27:26.880 --> 00:27:28.840
within an Istio
environment, or actually

00:27:28.840 --> 00:27:31.560
within an Envoy environment.

00:27:31.560 --> 00:27:32.970
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: Sounds good.

00:27:32.970 --> 00:27:35.470
[LAUGHTER]

00:27:35.470 --> 00:27:39.480
So actually, coming
back to you Matt,

00:27:39.480 --> 00:27:44.460
i just brushed through a lot
of technical details of Envoy.

00:27:44.460 --> 00:27:47.190
I think a lot of people may
not be familiar with things

00:27:47.190 --> 00:27:50.340
such as filters, or I
know that observability

00:27:50.340 --> 00:27:51.600
is built into Envoy.

00:27:51.600 --> 00:27:55.460
Can you describe, let's
say, your top five features

00:27:55.460 --> 00:27:58.950
for which you see all
your users using Envoy?

00:27:58.950 --> 00:28:00.000
MATT KLEIN: Sure.

00:28:00.000 --> 00:28:02.970
Yeah, I think that's a
common question recently,

00:28:02.970 --> 00:28:06.120
which is why has Envoy
become so popular?

00:28:06.120 --> 00:28:08.230
I think there's a couple
of different reasons.

00:28:08.230 --> 00:28:13.740
I think the API is probably
the number one reason.

00:28:13.740 --> 00:28:16.510
Actually, when I look
at, for my opinion,

00:28:16.510 --> 00:28:18.860
one of the biggest
technology successes

00:28:18.860 --> 00:28:22.470
I've seen in the last couple
of years, it's actually Proto3.

00:28:22.470 --> 00:28:25.860
Proto3, in my m has
been pretty awesome,

00:28:25.860 --> 00:28:28.110
just in the sense
that the ability

00:28:28.110 --> 00:28:33.370
to go from a structured IDL to
JSON YAML and back and forth.

00:28:33.370 --> 00:28:36.120
And that's how we do our
new configuration now.

00:28:36.120 --> 00:28:39.000
So the API is the
same as the config,

00:28:39.000 --> 00:28:40.860
and it's super seamless.

00:28:40.860 --> 00:28:44.040
You can use GRPC in
a different language,

00:28:44.040 --> 00:28:48.120
you can write config
files in YAML and JSON,

00:28:48.120 --> 00:28:49.870
and it all works the same way.

00:28:49.870 --> 00:28:51.760
And I think people
really love that.

00:28:51.760 --> 00:28:55.290
So the fact that you
can control Envoy either

00:28:55.290 --> 00:28:58.200
through local config files
or you can serve everything

00:28:58.200 --> 00:29:02.100
over a central management
server, it's super flexible.

00:29:02.100 --> 00:29:06.660
So, I think, historically, with
proxies like NGINX and HAProxy,

00:29:06.660 --> 00:29:09.600
where people have had to
ship around config files--

00:29:09.600 --> 00:29:13.530
and that works fine, but
at large scale trying

00:29:13.530 --> 00:29:16.590
to manage those files, and make
sure the updates take place,

00:29:16.590 --> 00:29:18.480
and kind of figure
out how to do that

00:29:18.480 --> 00:29:21.610
from a deployment perspective
can be quite complicated.

00:29:21.610 --> 00:29:24.090
So the API has
been very flexible

00:29:24.090 --> 00:29:26.760
and has been super popular.

00:29:26.760 --> 00:29:30.220
The other thing is the
extension capabilities.

00:29:30.220 --> 00:29:34.310
So when I first designed
Envoy, I kind of

00:29:34.310 --> 00:29:36.310
based it on prior experience.

00:29:36.310 --> 00:29:39.390
And instead of making
it very HTTP-specific,

00:29:39.390 --> 00:29:44.640
I built it in a way where
it was an L3, L4 proxy.

00:29:44.640 --> 00:29:46.630
So basically,
bytes in bytes out.

00:29:46.630 --> 00:29:49.170
And that means that it could
be used for other protocols.

00:29:49.170 --> 00:29:53.440
So at Lyft today we use it for
MongoDB, we use it for Redis.

00:29:53.440 --> 00:29:57.100
There's people now building
thrift support, mySQL,

00:29:57.100 --> 00:29:59.376
we'll do Kafka eventually.

00:29:59.376 --> 00:30:00.750
And what you'll
find is that even

00:30:00.750 --> 00:30:02.580
in all of these protocol
implementations,

00:30:02.580 --> 00:30:05.400
there's a lot of common
core stuff around service

00:30:05.400 --> 00:30:08.590
discovery, health checking.

00:30:08.590 --> 00:30:10.890
So lots of common
functionality can be shared,

00:30:10.890 --> 00:30:13.770
and then you can build
these protocols on top.

00:30:13.770 --> 00:30:17.100
And then we also allow
extensions at the HTTP layer,

00:30:17.100 --> 00:30:21.360
at the stats layer,
at the tracing layer.

00:30:21.360 --> 00:30:23.660
So it's a pretty
rich platform from

00:30:23.660 --> 00:30:25.690
an extensibility perspective.

00:30:25.690 --> 00:30:28.980
So I think from a
technology perspective,

00:30:28.980 --> 00:30:31.340
those are the two main reasons.

00:30:31.340 --> 00:30:35.760
A third reason is just from
a developer-productivity

00:30:35.760 --> 00:30:37.770
perspective, I'll
be honest in saying

00:30:37.770 --> 00:30:42.660
that we're the first open source
proxy where we're on GitHub,

00:30:42.660 --> 00:30:45.642
we do CI, we have a
very open community.

00:30:45.642 --> 00:30:47.100
So I think people
have really loved

00:30:47.100 --> 00:30:51.120
the fact that we're very easy to
engage with and get patches in.

00:30:51.120 --> 00:30:54.990
So I think those are
the big three reasons.

00:30:54.990 --> 00:30:59.130
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: And Anna,
it is similar to how Matt

00:30:59.130 --> 00:31:01.680
did a little bit of deep dive
into some of the features,

00:31:01.680 --> 00:31:05.280
can you, whenever we say, oh,
we have global load balancing,

00:31:05.280 --> 00:31:07.820
for a lot of people it's
this vision of a box--

00:31:07.820 --> 00:31:10.960
like a global box-- which is
thread like this huge thing.

00:31:10.960 --> 00:31:13.030
I know it's not that at all.

00:31:13.030 --> 00:31:15.020
Can you describe a
little bit about,

00:31:15.020 --> 00:31:17.760
what is this Traffic Director
thing under the hood?

00:31:17.760 --> 00:31:21.450
What does the
architecture look like?

00:31:21.450 --> 00:31:24.540
ANNA BERENBERG: So if you
think about Traffic Director it

00:31:24.540 --> 00:31:27.990
has two interfaces, one
interface to the proxy,

00:31:27.990 --> 00:31:30.960
and it's open XDS APIs.

00:31:30.960 --> 00:31:34.420
And they have several
APIs in them--

00:31:34.420 --> 00:31:39.300
cluster discovery service,
endpoint discovery service,

00:31:39.300 --> 00:31:42.380
listener discovery service,
route discovery service,

00:31:42.380 --> 00:31:44.620
health discovery
service eventually,

00:31:44.620 --> 00:31:48.990
load reporting service,
secret discovery service.

00:31:48.990 --> 00:31:50.010
I'm running it.

00:31:50.010 --> 00:31:53.820
So basically it's
all the communication

00:31:53.820 --> 00:31:59.980
that needs to happen between
the proxy and the control plane.

00:31:59.980 --> 00:32:04.230
Now, on the backside,
Traffic Director

00:32:04.230 --> 00:32:07.500
has interfaces to
Google systems--

00:32:07.500 --> 00:32:10.800
our proprietary
systems-- that actually

00:32:10.800 --> 00:32:13.000
do all that functionality.

00:32:13.000 --> 00:32:16.500
We have a control system that
has global load balancing

00:32:16.500 --> 00:32:20.190
in a way that there is
nothing in the industry where

00:32:20.190 --> 00:32:25.170
we actually load balance based
on a capacity of the back-end.

00:32:25.170 --> 00:32:31.185
So we don't do spray and pray,
we actually count the queries.

00:32:33.940 --> 00:32:37.290
And so we will never deliver
more than the customer wanted

00:32:37.290 --> 00:32:38.760
us to deliver.

00:32:38.760 --> 00:32:40.080
And then we have autoscale.

00:32:40.080 --> 00:32:44.380
For example, based
on that capacity.

00:32:44.380 --> 00:32:48.720
So in addition, we do
centralized health checking.

00:32:48.720 --> 00:32:50.730
So what is centralized
health checking?

00:32:50.730 --> 00:32:56.070
When I joined Google, one
of the biggest consumers

00:32:56.070 --> 00:32:58.230
in Google cluster was
a health checking,

00:32:58.230 --> 00:33:01.590
because when the
deployments get big,

00:33:01.590 --> 00:33:03.840
if you start doing
[INAUDIBLE] health checking,

00:33:03.840 --> 00:33:07.410
the bandwidth consumed by
health checks is enormous.

00:33:07.410 --> 00:33:10.780
So all of that sounds like,
eh, that's not the problem.

00:33:10.780 --> 00:33:13.020
It actually becomes very boring.

00:33:13.020 --> 00:33:15.760
Boring health checking
becomes a massive problem,

00:33:15.760 --> 00:33:18.010
because it consumes
all of your network.

00:33:18.010 --> 00:33:20.280
So centralized health
checking allows you

00:33:20.280 --> 00:33:23.760
to dedicate health
checker and health check

00:33:23.760 --> 00:33:31.320
all back-ends in a way that
it's actually trust the result.

00:33:31.320 --> 00:33:33.390
The results are getting
merged and aggregated

00:33:33.390 --> 00:33:34.740
by control plane.

00:33:34.740 --> 00:33:37.230
And then they are distributed
with endpoint discovery

00:33:37.230 --> 00:33:38.790
service.

00:33:38.790 --> 00:33:40.120
Same with auto scaling--

00:33:40.120 --> 00:33:45.370
we take all that data reported
through load reporting service,

00:33:45.370 --> 00:33:49.500
and we digest it, and we
translate it into the demand

00:33:49.500 --> 00:33:51.900
that autoscaler
needs to get out.

00:33:51.900 --> 00:33:54.420
And we integrate
it with autoscaler,

00:33:54.420 --> 00:33:59.520
and the autoscaler scales
up based on traffic demand.

00:33:59.520 --> 00:34:03.870
And the funny thing that I was
telling Matt, our internal API

00:34:03.870 --> 00:34:08.659
is actually matched very
closely to the Envoy APIs,

00:34:08.659 --> 00:34:11.820
and that persuaded me that
that's the right proxy to use.

00:34:14.354 --> 00:34:15.330
MATT KLEIN: Yeah.

00:34:15.330 --> 00:34:18.300
I think that, when
most companies look

00:34:18.300 --> 00:34:21.690
at deploying a proxy, like
Envoy, or like HAProxy,

00:34:21.690 --> 00:34:26.159
or NGINX, most companies
start with a fairly simplistic

00:34:26.159 --> 00:34:27.300
control plane.

00:34:27.300 --> 00:34:29.429
And if you even look at
what Lyft does compared

00:34:29.429 --> 00:34:31.320
to what Google
does, it's obviously

00:34:31.320 --> 00:34:33.790
a totally different
magnitude of scale.

00:34:33.790 --> 00:34:36.810
So it's not surprising that
we do things in a far less

00:34:36.810 --> 00:34:38.860
sophisticated way.

00:34:38.860 --> 00:34:43.020
But what I'm so excited about,
just this whole general area,

00:34:43.020 --> 00:34:48.150
is that because of this open
API, and the fact that people

00:34:48.150 --> 00:34:52.170
are converging on this API, it
will allow companies like Lyft,

00:34:52.170 --> 00:34:55.230
eventually, who, frankly,
are not going to be able

00:34:55.230 --> 00:34:58.620
or really can justify
building a control plane as

00:34:58.620 --> 00:35:01.110
sophisticated as what
Google can offer,

00:35:01.110 --> 00:35:03.400
we can just go and use
that control plane.

00:35:03.400 --> 00:35:05.820
And I think there's tons
of companies out there

00:35:05.820 --> 00:35:09.000
that would benefit from
this type of technology.

00:35:09.000 --> 00:35:14.910
And from a Lyft perspective, we
can still run our own Envoys,

00:35:14.910 --> 00:35:18.030
and still manage them, and
still do what we want with them,

00:35:18.030 --> 00:35:22.050
but we can get far more
sophisticated functionality

00:35:22.050 --> 00:35:24.300
from a health
checking perspective,

00:35:24.300 --> 00:35:26.366
from a subsetting perspective,
than we would ever

00:35:26.366 --> 00:35:27.490
be able to build ourselves.

00:35:27.490 --> 00:35:31.190
So that's super exciting.

00:35:31.190 --> 00:35:34.180
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: That's awesome.

00:35:34.180 --> 00:35:35.990
While both of you
are speaking, you

00:35:35.990 --> 00:35:39.020
were talking about Google
and Lyft, a lot of people

00:35:39.020 --> 00:35:43.580
hear have their own deployments,
which likely are on-prem.

00:35:43.580 --> 00:35:46.910
Many of you are moving
or have moved to GCP.

00:35:46.910 --> 00:35:49.420
And a lot of your services
are probably in VMs,

00:35:49.420 --> 00:35:52.730
but you do want to containerize
them to get all the benefits.

00:35:52.730 --> 00:35:54.740
And then in all
of this, we added

00:35:54.740 --> 00:35:58.310
things like on Envoy proxy,
Istio, Traffic Director.

00:35:58.310 --> 00:36:01.480
So for people who are
starting out here,

00:36:01.480 --> 00:36:04.250
at least based on your
experience with your users,

00:36:04.250 --> 00:36:06.750
how do people bring in
these new technologies

00:36:06.750 --> 00:36:10.250
into brownfield or
existing deployments?

00:36:10.250 --> 00:36:12.720
MATT KLEIN: That's tough.

00:36:12.720 --> 00:36:16.550
And it really depends on
the particular deployment.

00:36:16.550 --> 00:36:19.790
I think what's interesting
from an Envoy perspective,

00:36:19.790 --> 00:36:24.020
is that there is such a
rich ecosystem of projects

00:36:24.020 --> 00:36:26.070
that are kind of starting
to be built on top.

00:36:26.070 --> 00:36:28.310
So obviously, Istio
is super well-known,

00:36:28.310 --> 00:36:29.990
there's a bunch
of other companies

00:36:29.990 --> 00:36:32.420
that are building stuff on top.

00:36:32.420 --> 00:36:35.360
So kind of what I tell
people these days,

00:36:35.360 --> 00:36:37.970
at least from an Envoy or
from a proxy perspective,

00:36:37.970 --> 00:36:41.390
is that I would not recommend
that companies go and build

00:36:41.390 --> 00:36:42.560
their own control plane.

00:36:42.560 --> 00:36:45.995
I think, at this point, people
should be looking at Istio,

00:36:45.995 --> 00:36:48.800
looking at pilot, looking
at other solutions that

00:36:48.800 --> 00:36:52.880
are out there, just
because I think

00:36:52.880 --> 00:36:56.452
people underestimate the
complexity of building

00:36:56.452 --> 00:36:57.410
your own control plane.

00:36:57.410 --> 00:36:58.850
I think people look at
it, and they're like, oh,

00:36:58.850 --> 00:36:59.850
how hard can it be?

00:36:59.850 --> 00:37:03.140
I can get some host information,
and send it to Envoy,

00:37:03.140 --> 00:37:04.760
and things will just work.

00:37:04.760 --> 00:37:07.310
And it does do that
in the beginning,

00:37:07.310 --> 00:37:09.680
but then as you start
scaling up and you

00:37:09.680 --> 00:37:11.930
deal with the [INAUDIBLE]
health checking problem,

00:37:11.930 --> 00:37:15.260
and you deal with security,
and service manifests, and all

00:37:15.260 --> 00:37:18.360
this other stuff, it gets
complicated really quick.

00:37:18.360 --> 00:37:21.110
So I think my
recommendation, right now

00:37:21.110 --> 00:37:24.020
for people that are looking
into these solutions,

00:37:24.020 --> 00:37:28.550
is to not necessarily even
look at Envoy directly,

00:37:28.550 --> 00:37:30.530
but to look at some of
the stuff that people

00:37:30.530 --> 00:37:32.160
are building on top.

00:37:32.160 --> 00:37:34.250
And one of the things
I've been saying recently,

00:37:34.250 --> 00:37:36.980
and I really firmly
believe, is that it's

00:37:36.980 --> 00:37:39.500
really amazing the Envoy
has become so popular

00:37:39.500 --> 00:37:43.460
and it's getting very wide
uptake, but at the same time,

00:37:43.460 --> 00:37:46.175
I think that in a couple of
years though Envoy will be used

00:37:46.175 --> 00:37:48.050
in a lot of places, I
think most people won't

00:37:48.050 --> 00:37:49.520
know that they're using Envoy.

00:37:49.520 --> 00:37:52.250
I think they're going to consume
it as part of other products

00:37:52.250 --> 00:37:54.530
or other systems.

00:37:54.530 --> 00:37:56.330
So I would definitely
encourage folks

00:37:56.330 --> 00:38:00.639
to look at some of the systems
that are being built on top.

00:38:00.639 --> 00:38:02.180
PRAJAKTA JOSHI:
Anna, what about you?

00:38:02.180 --> 00:38:03.590
What do you think--

00:38:03.590 --> 00:38:05.840
I mean, we hear
this all the time

00:38:05.840 --> 00:38:09.320
if you have customers coming
in with existing deployments.

00:38:09.320 --> 00:38:11.330
And let's say it's
somebody who is

00:38:11.330 --> 00:38:13.430
running their service in a VM.

00:38:13.430 --> 00:38:15.440
How would you ask them to start?

00:38:15.440 --> 00:38:17.360
Or where would they start?

00:38:17.360 --> 00:38:19.550
ANNA BERENBERG: So
from our perspective,

00:38:19.550 --> 00:38:22.730
the configuration coming
to Traffic Director

00:38:22.730 --> 00:38:26.340
is done via GCP API.

00:38:26.340 --> 00:38:30.740
And in the future, we will
add additional Istio APIs.

00:38:30.740 --> 00:38:34.790
But GCP API is
already integrated--

00:38:34.790 --> 00:38:38.600
those are load balancing
APIs, you provision other load

00:38:38.600 --> 00:38:39.650
balancing product.

00:38:39.650 --> 00:38:44.360
And what we have done, we
use the same data model

00:38:44.360 --> 00:38:47.420
to provision Traffic
Director and Envoys.

00:38:47.420 --> 00:38:50.150
The only difference
is one boolean.

00:38:50.150 --> 00:38:54.910
And so, in my mind, one, if
you're already using GCP,

00:38:54.910 --> 00:38:57.980
it's easier to
add-on, because it's

00:38:57.980 --> 00:39:02.980
just you add one boolean
and instantiation of Envoy.

00:39:05.800 --> 00:39:09.250
Also, because it's
all integrated,

00:39:09.250 --> 00:39:13.210
Traffic Director essentially
becomes a centralized brain

00:39:13.210 --> 00:39:16.090
for all your
[INAUDIBLE] everywhere.

00:39:16.090 --> 00:39:21.910
So whether you're configuring
Envoy, which is self-managed,

00:39:21.910 --> 00:39:25.570
or maybe manager, or
some other product that

00:39:25.570 --> 00:39:29.710
is going to be using Envoy, or
for that intent and purposes,

00:39:29.710 --> 00:39:33.560
any proxy that's
using the same APIs,

00:39:33.560 --> 00:39:37.210
we can extend your
existing deployment

00:39:37.210 --> 00:39:42.660
to Istio or to just Envoy.

00:39:42.660 --> 00:39:44.850
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: Sounds good.

00:39:44.850 --> 00:39:46.560
I actually wanted to give--

00:39:46.560 --> 00:39:48.370
because we want
to hear from you,

00:39:48.370 --> 00:39:51.820
so I'm going to give you
this hypothetical scenario.

00:39:51.820 --> 00:39:53.871
Let's think, let's say
three years down the line.

00:39:53.871 --> 00:39:54.870
I don't like five years.

00:39:54.870 --> 00:39:58.400
We'll go with three years.

00:39:58.400 --> 00:40:00.930
Would we be talking so
much about pinpoints

00:40:00.930 --> 00:40:02.310
of delivering hybrid services?

00:40:02.310 --> 00:40:05.850
Or do you think they'll be a
slew of products and platforms,

00:40:05.850 --> 00:40:07.860
with this being a
beginning, where

00:40:07.860 --> 00:40:12.060
it's just natural to have
something that's across clouds?

00:40:12.060 --> 00:40:14.310
How do you see the world,
maybe three years from now--

00:40:14.310 --> 00:40:16.067
what kind of products,
technologies,

00:40:16.067 --> 00:40:18.150
what is the next thing you
will be working on that

00:40:18.150 --> 00:40:21.650
will help some of this stuff?

00:40:21.650 --> 00:40:25.150
MATT KLEIN: So from a
three-year perspective,

00:40:25.150 --> 00:40:26.260
that's tough to say.

00:40:26.260 --> 00:40:28.990
But I'll talk a little
more I think in the 5

00:40:28.990 --> 00:40:31.690
to 10-year timeframe,
just in the sense

00:40:31.690 --> 00:40:36.670
that I'm a big believer in
serverless technologies.

00:40:36.670 --> 00:40:40.660
Serverless just in the sense
that I think most application

00:40:40.660 --> 00:40:43.120
developers, they want
to write business logic.

00:40:43.120 --> 00:40:45.160
They don't want to worry
about infrastructure.

00:40:45.160 --> 00:40:47.680
And the biggest thing that I
see in the industry right now

00:40:47.680 --> 00:40:50.470
is that most companies
are spending too much time

00:40:50.470 --> 00:40:54.460
on a per developer basis
worrying about infrastructure,

00:40:54.460 --> 00:40:57.100
worrying about how networking
works, or observability,

00:40:57.100 --> 00:40:58.900
or databases.

00:40:58.900 --> 00:41:02.740
So I'm a big believer in the
idea of eventually people

00:41:02.740 --> 00:41:04.600
writing functions
or code snippets

00:41:04.600 --> 00:41:07.510
and eventually being
able to just ship those.

00:41:07.510 --> 00:41:10.480
With that said, I think we're
very, very far from there,

00:41:10.480 --> 00:41:13.170
just because I think a
lot of those technologies,

00:41:13.170 --> 00:41:15.730
they're orders of
magnitude more complicated

00:41:15.730 --> 00:41:17.766
than what we have today.

00:41:17.766 --> 00:41:19.390
There's just there's
more auto scaling,

00:41:19.390 --> 00:41:24.010
there's more networking, there's
more problems that can happen.

00:41:24.010 --> 00:41:27.970
So where I see
things going is, I

00:41:27.970 --> 00:41:30.430
think that, increasingly
all of the cloud

00:41:30.430 --> 00:41:33.130
providers, not just
Google, are going

00:41:33.130 --> 00:41:36.250
to start offering more
integrated products, where

00:41:36.250 --> 00:41:37.960
you might not call
it serverless--

00:41:37.960 --> 00:41:40.060
it might be container-based.

00:41:40.060 --> 00:41:42.850
But the idea is that
people are going

00:41:42.850 --> 00:41:45.220
to expect a certain
set of technologies

00:41:45.220 --> 00:41:49.270
around observability, service
discovery, load balancing, rate

00:41:49.270 --> 00:41:52.350
limiting, policy, RBAC.

00:41:52.350 --> 00:41:55.000
And instead of having to worry
about how it's implemented,

00:41:55.000 --> 00:41:57.190
people just want to
show up, and say,

00:41:57.190 --> 00:41:59.120
I'm going to use these features.

00:41:59.120 --> 00:42:00.610
So I'm biased.

00:42:00.610 --> 00:42:05.872
I think Envoy will be used
to deliver those use cases.

00:42:05.872 --> 00:42:07.330
But as I was saying
before, I don't

00:42:07.330 --> 00:42:09.788
think many people will end up
knowing that its Envoy that's

00:42:09.788 --> 00:42:10.630
actually doing that.

00:42:10.630 --> 00:42:12.910
They're going to use
integrated products.

00:42:12.910 --> 00:42:15.400
So right now, from a
hybrid perspective,

00:42:15.400 --> 00:42:18.500
obviously, we're going to
have years of people who have

00:42:18.500 --> 00:42:21.190
on-prem, or in multiple clouds.

00:42:21.190 --> 00:42:23.830
But over time, I
think we're going

00:42:23.830 --> 00:42:25.780
to move to hosted Kubernetes.

00:42:25.780 --> 00:42:28.780
And then from there we're going
to move to hosted function

00:42:28.780 --> 00:42:29.470
platforms.

00:42:29.470 --> 00:42:32.170
And it's going to
be a slow iteration,

00:42:32.170 --> 00:42:34.210
but we just keep adding
more abstractions,

00:42:34.210 --> 00:42:37.150
and hopefully every year
people will have to deal less

00:42:37.150 --> 00:42:38.374
with infrastructure.

00:42:40.365 --> 00:42:41.990
ANNA BERENBERG: I
probably want to talk

00:42:41.990 --> 00:42:44.600
about an even higher
level of abstraction.

00:42:44.600 --> 00:42:48.560
I think in three
years, maybe in five,

00:42:48.560 --> 00:42:52.940
people stop configuring system
as they are today via APIs,

00:42:52.940 --> 00:42:59.360
via these YAML files, and
everything, because we need

00:42:59.360 --> 00:43:04.820
to not make the
service owners to be

00:43:04.820 --> 00:43:08.780
having PhD in provisioning.

00:43:08.780 --> 00:43:12.910
So all we want to do
is actually to move

00:43:12.910 --> 00:43:16.010
towards intent-driven
configuration,

00:43:16.010 --> 00:43:20.450
where the service can be
described into intents.

00:43:20.450 --> 00:43:24.540
Do you want to
have a service with

00:43:24.540 --> 00:43:27.410
latency-optimized to
optimized perceived

00:43:27.410 --> 00:43:34.610
user latency from the user to
the front-end overall serving?

00:43:34.610 --> 00:43:38.660
Or the other one, do you want
to have cost-optimized service,

00:43:38.660 --> 00:43:43.160
or do you want to optimize
towards transferring less date

00:43:43.160 --> 00:43:45.680
are over wire, or
something else?

00:43:45.680 --> 00:43:50.000
These are the configurations
that will internally translate

00:43:50.000 --> 00:43:51.350
into write configuration.

00:43:51.350 --> 00:43:53.330
Because, today with
the hundreds of knobs,

00:43:53.330 --> 00:43:57.710
even with your best intent you
may not end up with the intent

00:43:57.710 --> 00:43:59.250
you want to have.

00:43:59.250 --> 00:44:02.900
So we need to be able to
express intent very clearly

00:44:02.900 --> 00:44:05.360
in a high level and
translate it internally.

00:44:05.360 --> 00:44:07.230
And the second
thing, I think it's

00:44:07.230 --> 00:44:11.690
super important to stop
thinking about each microservice

00:44:11.690 --> 00:44:12.530
separately.

00:44:12.530 --> 00:44:16.070
We need to start thinking
about overall deployment

00:44:16.070 --> 00:44:22.820
and how to have
configuration policies that

00:44:22.820 --> 00:44:26.450
apply to overall deployment,
how to provide consistency

00:44:26.450 --> 00:44:29.390
and coherency to make sure
that if you're talking

00:44:29.390 --> 00:44:32.450
about security policies, you
don't have to go and configure

00:44:32.450 --> 00:44:35.210
on each of 180 microservices.

00:44:35.210 --> 00:44:38.390
All you have to say, I'm
going to provision it one way,

00:44:38.390 --> 00:44:39.860
and then it's
going to understand

00:44:39.860 --> 00:44:42.470
how to apply it to everywhere
in the different ones,

00:44:42.470 --> 00:44:45.080
however service is deployed.

00:44:45.080 --> 00:44:52.100
And then you can link it into
some recommendation engine

00:44:52.100 --> 00:44:54.590
that that can say,
oh, my god, you

00:44:54.590 --> 00:44:58.490
didn't provision
something somewhere,

00:44:58.490 --> 00:45:01.850
or you have vulnerability
about security.

00:45:01.850 --> 00:45:07.100
Or, in general, it can look at
the deployment, and can say,

00:45:07.100 --> 00:45:08.131
you can do better.

00:45:08.131 --> 00:45:08.630
Right?

00:45:08.630 --> 00:45:11.900
That's I think where we want
to be in three, five years.

00:45:14.487 --> 00:45:16.320
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: So I
think with that we come

00:45:16.320 --> 00:45:18.120
to a close of this session.

00:45:18.120 --> 00:45:21.270
Matt and Ann will be here,
probably outside the room.

00:45:21.270 --> 00:45:23.270
So feel free to reach out.

00:45:23.270 --> 00:45:24.960
The nice thing
is, a lot of these

00:45:24.960 --> 00:45:27.630
are very new technologies,
and the reason

00:45:27.630 --> 00:45:29.520
we are talking
about them so early

00:45:29.520 --> 00:45:31.920
is because we do
want each one of you

00:45:31.920 --> 00:45:33.690
to influence these technologies.

00:45:33.690 --> 00:45:35.370
And we added a set
of features that we

00:45:35.370 --> 00:45:36.580
thought were table stakes.

00:45:36.580 --> 00:45:39.270
But I'm sure there are a lot
more features that you need

00:45:39.270 --> 00:45:41.580
on your end to make
this just be the tool

00:45:41.580 --> 00:45:44.070
to make your hybrid and
multicloud deployments

00:45:44.070 --> 00:45:44.751
seamless.

00:45:44.751 --> 00:45:46.500
So with that, thank
you, folks for coming.

00:45:46.500 --> 00:45:49.050
And feel free to talk
to our two people

00:45:49.050 --> 00:45:51.070
and ask them all
the tough questions.

00:45:51.070 --> 00:45:52.220
Thank you everyone.

00:45:52.220 --> 00:45:55.870
[MUSIC PLAYING]

